  "content": {
    "equation": "Morphine Milligram Equivalent per day [MMED] for each drug = (Total daily opioid dose) *\n(Conversion factor)\n• See table below for drug-specific conversion factors\nTotal MMED = sum of all individual drug MMEDs (calculated in morphine milligram equivalents per\nday)\nDrug\nMorphine Milligram Equivalent (MME) Conversion\nFactora\nCodeine (mg)\n0.15\nDihydrocodeine (mg)\n0.25\nFentaNYL (transdermal patch)\n(mcg/hour)b\n2.4\nHYDROcodone (mg)\n1\nHYDROmorphone (mg)\n5\nLevorphanol tartrate (mg)\n11\nMethadone (mg)c,d\n>0 to ≤20\n4\n>20 to ≤40\n8\n>20 to ≤40\n8\n>40 to ≤60\n10\n>60\n12\nMorphine (mg)\n1\nOxyCODONE (mg)\n1.5\nOxyMORphone (mg)\n3\nPentazocine (mg)\n0.37\nTapentadol (mg)e\n0.4\nTraMADol (mg)e\n0.2\na Note: Published conversion factors are estimates, and do not account for individual differences\nin drug metabolism or pharmacokinetics (CDC [Dowell 2022]; Von Korff 2008).\nb The MME conversion factor for fentanyl patches is based on the assumptions that 1 mg (1,000\nmcg) of parenteral fentanyl is equivalent to 100 mg of oral morphine, and that one patch delivers\nthe dispensed mcg/hour over a 24-hour day.\nc MMED risk evaluation thresholds should not be applied to dosing decisions regarding\nmedications for opioid use disorder (eg, methadone) (CDC [Dowell 2022]).\nd Some publications list the methadone conversion factor as 4.7 (CDC [Dowell 2022]). However,\ncalculating MMED for methadone in practice often involves a sliding-scale approach whereby the\nconversion factor increases with increasing dose to avoid underestimating MMED. Centers for\nMedicare & Medicaid Services (and this calculator) uses the graduated methadone MME\nconversion factors to calculate MMED within the Overutilization Monitoring System (OMS) for\nidentifying and reporting potential opioid overutilizers. Available at https://medicaid-\ndocuments.dhhs.utah.gov/Documents/files/Opioid-Morphine-EQ-Conversion-Factors.pdf.\ne Tapentadol and tramadol are mixed mechanism opioids with weaker μ-receptor agonist activity\nthan pure μ-receptor agonists. MMEs are based on degree of μ-receptor agonist activity; however,\nit is unknown whether these drugs are associated with overdose in the same dose-dependent\nmanner as pure μ-receptor agonists (CDC [Dowell 2022]).",
    "notes": [],
    "conditional_notes": null,
    "calc_details": null,
    "data_input_disclaimer": "ONLY DIGITS 0 TO 9 AND A SINGLE DECIMAL POINT \".\" ARE ACCEPTABLE AS NUMERIC INPUTS. ATTEMPTED INPUT OF OTHER CHARACTERS INTO A NUMERIC FIELD MAY LEAD TO AN INCORRECT RESULT.",
