    "disclaimer": null,
    "copyright": null,
    "instructions": "To perform a calculation, enter the required variables. Once all the inputs are entered, click the \"Calculate\" button to perform the calculation. Any time an input variable is adjusted, the \"Calculate\" button must be clicked to populate the new output. To start over, click the \"Reset\" button to clear all values and begin the calculation again. Estimation of morphine milligram equivalents per day (MMED) is one important component in the identification of patients receiving opioid therapy who may be at a higher risk of opioid- induced respiratory depression and death (eg, patients receiving ≥50 oral MMED) and may help to determine who may benefit from closer monitoring, reduction or tapering of opioids, prescribing of naloxone, and other safeguards (CDC [Dowell 2022]). Other patient-specific factors besides MMED may also increase the risk of opioid-induced respiratory depression and death in patients receiving opioid therapy, including age, concomitant use of sedative- hypnotic agents (eg, benzodiazepines, skeletal muscle relaxants), and duration of opioid use (Garg 2017; King 2014). Common risk mitigation strategies include opioid management plans, patient education, urine drug screening, use of prescription drug monitoring program data, more frequent monitoring, pill counts, use of abuse-deterrent opioid formulations, and co- prescription of naloxone, among others (CDC [Dowell 2022]).",
